{
  "title": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic — Every Number's DNA 🧬",
  "hook": "Just like every person has a unique fingerprint, <strong>every number has a unique prime factorization</strong>. The number 360 will always be 2³ × 3² × 5, no matter how you factor it. This 'uniqueness' is so important, mathematicians call it the <em>Fundamental</em> Theorem!",
  "explanation": "<strong style=\"color:#C62828\">The Theorem:</strong> Every composite number can be expressed as a product of prime numbers, and this factorization is <strong>unique</strong> (apart from the order of factors).\n\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\">\n<div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">UNIQUE PRIME FACTORIZATION</div><strong>360 = 2³ × 3² × 5¹ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (always, no matter how you factor!)</strong></div>\n<strong>Building a Factor Tree:</strong>\n\n<div style=\"text-align:center;margin:12px 0\"><svg width=\"240\" height=\"180\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><text x=\"120\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"14\">360</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"48\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"48\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#C62828\" font-weight=\"bold\">2</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\">180</text><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#C62828\" font-weight=\"bold\">2</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\">90</text><line x1=\"205\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"136\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"235\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"136\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#C62828\" font-weight=\"bold\">2</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\">45 = 3² × 5</text></svg></div>\n\n<strong>Using Prime Factorization to Find HCF and LCM:</strong>\n\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\"></th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Method</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Example: HCF & LCM of 12 and 18</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\"><strong>HCF</strong></td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Product of <strong>common</strong> primes with <strong>lowest</strong> powers</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">12 = 2² × 3, 18 = 2 × 3²<br>Common: 2¹ × 3¹ = <strong>6</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\"><strong>LCM</strong></td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Product of <strong>all</strong> primes with <strong>highest</strong> powers</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">All primes: 2² × 3² = <strong>36</strong></td></tr></table><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\">\n<div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">GOLDEN RULE</div><strong>HCF × LCM = Product of the two numbers &nbsp;&nbsp; (6 × 36 = 216 = 12 × 18 ✓)</strong></div><div style=\"background:#FFF3E0;border-left:4px solid #E65100;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">⚠️ <strong>Tip:</strong> This HCF × LCM = Product rule works only for <strong>two</strong> numbers, not three or more!</div>",
  "worked_example": "<strong>Find HCF and LCM of 96 and 404:</strong>\n\n<div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#6A1B9A;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div>\n<div><strong>Prime factorize 96</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">96 = 2⁵ × 3</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#6A1B9A;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div>\n<div><strong>Prime factorize 404</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">404 = 2² × 101</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#6A1B9A;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div>\n<div><strong>HCF = common primes, lowest powers</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Common prime: 2. Lowest power: 2².<br><strong>HCF = 4</strong></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#6A1B9A;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div>\n<div><strong>LCM = all primes, highest powers</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">2⁵ × 3 × 101 = 32 × 3 × 101<br><strong>LCM = 9696</strong></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#6A1B9A;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">5</div>\n<div><strong>Verify</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">HCF × LCM = 4 × 9696 = 38784<br>96 × 404 = 38784 ✓ 🎉</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Find the HCF and LCM of 306 and 657 using prime factorization. Verify using the formula HCF × LCM = Product.",
    "hint": "306 = 2 × 3² × 17 and 657 = 3 × 219. Keep factoring 219!",
    "answer": "306 = 2 × 3² × 17\n657 = 3 × 219 = 3 × 3 × 73 = 3² × 73\nHCF = 3² = 9 (common prime with lowest power)\nLCM = 2 × 3² × 17 × 73 = 22338\nVerify: 9 × 22338 = 201042 and 306 × 657 = 201042 ✓"
  },
  "fun_fact": "The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic was first stated by Euclid, but it was Carl Friedrich Gauss (called the 'Prince of Mathematics') who gave the first rigorous proof in 1801 — in his book 'Disquisitiones Arithmeticae', which he wrote when he was just 21! 📚"
}